  geopolitics: "The transmission chain is now complete from the physical end to the price end, and the price end broke a level that changes central-bank arithmetic rather than just charts. WTI settled at 101.410, up 3.51% on the day and 10.41% on the week; Brent settled at 109.860, up 3.72%, after touching 110 intraday for the first time since May. The physical driver is unambiguous and getting worse. CNBC counts at least nine commercial ships attacked near the Strait of Hormuz since Sunday as Iran tries to choke off crude exports, with Kpler putting flows through the strait at 9.4 million barrels a day in the week to Wednesday, roughly 70% of the pre-war average. Reporting from October 13 has at least twelve tankers hit in and around the strait in a single week against an IMO count of nine vessel-related incidents over the same period, the difference being verification standards rather than disagreement about direction. Two cargo ships sank this week; Iran's Revolutionary Guard claims a mine sank one, while US Central Command says commercial and energy traffic continues to move and disputes Iran's claims to control the strait. Tanker rates are at record highs. Two other fronts added to the move: Houthi missiles are landing on Saudi Arabia, and Ukraine is striking Russian refineries after the energy ceasefire Washington tried to broker between Kyiv and Moscow unravelled. Iran's escalation remains the answer to the US naval blockade reinstated in July - having lost its own exports, Tehran is stopping everyone else's. The bear case has not gone away but it is policy-driven and dated rather than physical, which is exactly why USOIL is a medium-conviction long and not a high-conviction one even above 100: Washington is reported to be considering letting legal oil move on previously sanctioned tankers, which would cut freight costs; Trump has ruled out a nuclear strike on Iran; the IEA and G7 emergency release programme is a scheduled multi-month supply response; and the IEA's October report has supply rebounding in 2027 to tip the market into surplus, with diesel the tightest part after combined Middle East and Russian export losses of 1.3 million barrels a day in September. Crude is still minus 0.49% on the month, so it has failed near these levels before. The FX read now runs in four directions rather than three, and the fourth is the new one. First, oil-up is Canadian-dollar-positive and that channel is not just live but confirmed by the governor: Macklem told an IMF and World Bank panel on October 16 that energy prices pose challenges for the rate outlook, that the Bank has looked through the increase but the longer it continues the harder that becomes, and that Canada has not raised rates yet. The price has cleared his Halifax threshold rather than merely approached it, and the October 28 Monetary Policy Report has to replace a 75 dollar crude assumption in public. That is the upgrade to plus 2. Second, oil-up is an inflation shock for the energy importers, and the gas complex amplified it this week rather than cushioning it: German gas is up 12.57% on the week at 89.23 EUR/MWh, EU gas up 7.50% at 87.55, UK gas up 6.83% at 216.40 GBp/thm. For the euro area that strengthens Lagarde's case that the Council can anticipate second-round effects, and for Germany it is a direct margin headwind that turns GER40 short. For the UK it restores the premise behind Bailey's late-September conditional one session after he softened it. Third, the haven channel stayed shut, and this time gold shut with it. For five weeks war headlines produced no franc bid; on Thursday a credit scare produced one; on Friday the franc kept a quiet bid against five of seven majors on the week while the SNB's December probability did not move a basis point, which marks it as flow rather than repricing - and the SNB is reported to regard the oil-driven rise in inflation as temporary, so an importer's central bank at zero has publicly declined to respond to the dominant shock. Gold, which held when the franc broke yesterday, failed its own test today: the dollar fell, the ten-year came down to 5.0850 and crude broke 100, and XAU/USD closed at 4,166.39, minus 0.11% on the day and minus 0.66% on the week. Three legs pointing one way and no bid is a reason to be flat, not long. The fourth direction is the one worth carrying forward, because it is where the oil shock stopped being a single trade and became a sorting mechanism. The same move that credited Canada debited Japan, which imports essentially all of its crude - and it did so on the very day Ueda said underlying inflation is already quite close to 2%, that the Bank cannot allow it to climb further, that rate rises will continue, and that he will discuss changing the approaching-2% language at the October 29-30 meeting. The yen fell against all seven majors anyway, because a terms-of-trade transfer clears in days and a sentence change clears on October 30. The same move also taxed copper, which fell 1.68% on the week in its steepest drop since May on exactly that logic - expensive crude is a cost input and a demand risk for industrial metal - and in doing so broke the Australian dollar's terms-of-trade leg one day after this file reported it repaired. So one shock produced a Canadian upgrade, a Japanese short, an Australian stall and a European equity headwind, and the honest way to trade it is pair by pair on the sign of the transmission rather than as a single risk-on or risk-off regime. The war is the reason crude is above 100 and therefore the reason the Canadian dollar sits at the top of this board. It is still not the reason the franc rallies, and as of today it is not the reason gold does either.",
